00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.360 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined today by Liz  suave Ola who is a senior program  associate at the Vera Institute of  justice and also one of the authors of  the recent report overlooked women in  jails in an era of reform Liz it's so  great to talk to you you know but  prisons and jails are often considered a  sort of men's issue the so-called men's  rights movement often talks about it in  that context but you studied another  angle of incarceration in the u.s. which  is that we've seen a 14 fold increase in  women in the correctional population  over the last just under 45 years what's  driving that increase let's start sort  of at the very top level the number of  women in jails that's local county  municipality run facilities and most of  those people are pretrial so they've  been arrested but they haven't been  convicted of anything they're weeding  the resolution of their cases and one  thing that was really surprising in our  research is that despite this dramatic  growth we don't still to this day know  what's really driving it because there  hasn't been much research specific to  women and what we were able to find in  our research about women in the colonel  justice system more generally is that  increasing focus on low-level nonviolent  offenses has really swept a large number  of women into the system and when we  talk about nonviolent offenses I mean I  think because of what we've experienced  within the war on drugs oftentimes  people's minds when they hear nonviolent  offenses they think of sort of drug use  for example possession etc but really  when we talk about nonviolent offenses  it includes more than just that doesn't  it I mean talk to us about some of the  other nonviolent offenses that are  landing people in jail in prison I'm so  not sales specifically for women and its  property offenses  Public Order offenses things like  loitering disorderly conduct I'm so  we're talking about really minor  low-level crimes talk to us a little bit  about the dynamic in terms of how the  criminal justice system sort of accounts  for the the reality that in the u.s.  women are more likely mothers are more  likely to be the caregivers for children  than our fathers and sort of how that is  impacting and influencing that the  numbers that we're seeing in terms of  female incarceration doesn't account for  that dynamic unfortunately about 80  percent of women in jails are mothers  oftentimes they are single mothers and  quite frequently when they are ending up  in the criminal justice system it's  related to efforts to kind of survive so  as we said it's property crimes it can  relate to mental health and behavioral  health and when they're coming into the  system that impacts their entire  families especially their children  visiting can be very difficult even  staying in touch by phone can be  prohibitively expensive many of these  families are poor and low-income and it  can be very difficult for for mother and  child to stay in touch when we think  about the incarceration of mothers  there's sort of this vicious cycle that  can develop where we know that a child  who has one or two parents that have  been in the system are those children  are more likely later on to end up in  the system themselves and there's this  this sort of vicious cycle that I've not  really heard a particularly good  explanation as to how it can be broken  but let me open up sort of that topic to  you first  talk a little bit about that right the  impact that the incarceration of mothers  has on the children and and whether you  want to talk short medium and law or  long term whichever way you want to go  with it  parent especially mother is incarcerated  there can be a whole host of  consequences I mean very from the very  first point of arrest that can be  incredibly traumatic for children that  can result in nightmares and stress and  trouble at school you know it's still an  area of emerging research really what  the long-term consequences are but  incarceration of children who have  parents who are incarcerated has been  you know strongly correlated we know  from having spoken to so many experts  around these issues that when someone is  incarcerated they're often to say  rehabilitated is very very far from the  truth they often come out of jail or  prison actually more disadvantaged less  likely to ultimately find stable  employment etc we know that generally  does your report tell us anything more  specifically about how that dynamic the  sort of furthering of the disadvantage  is affecting women who are incarcerated  absolutely they're really experiencing a  whole system with policies and practices  that are designed for men and that  really does please leave them worse off  than when they came into jail it can be  anything as basic as hygiene so having  access to feminine hygiene products  having access to toilet paper through to  trauma I mean the experience can be  incredibly traumatizing for women many  of whom have already experienced trauma  in their lifetime nearly 90 percent have  experienced sexual violence over the  course of their lifetime so when they  come to jail and they are searched or  shackled or held in solitary confinement  a lot of that trauma is read triggered  and it can really leave them worse off  as well as financial consequences many  women coming to jail are more  disadvantaged even than their male  counterparts so about 60% of women who  are in jail were unemployed before they  came to jail  and so when they  leaving jail and they have all of these  fines and fees that really rack up in  every single point of the criminal  justice process they are even further  economically disadvantaged this sort of  gets us to one of the the chicken-egg  discussions that often exists in these  conversations and I'll focus on the  unemployment aspect of it because you  brought it up some might hear this  scenario right they said they hear you  say incarceration rate of women has gone  up so significantly we also see 60% of  women coming into the system are  unemployed so we should really be  looking at arresting women less or  changing laws in order to reduce the  number of women arrested to begin with  others will hear it and they'll say well  the problem is really jobs right if we  were able to grow employment and make it  so that more women were employed and  addressed the issues that exists there  that's just going to naturally reduce  the number of women who end up in  situations where they end up getting  arrested is either of these approaches  does it does one or the other approach  make more sense to you is it parts of  both is it something else altogether is  really a result of poverty but also  increasingly becoming a cause of poverty  we have to look at the very beginning of  the system at arrests to think about  means that women can be diverted away  from the criminal justice system so that  they can engage in employment and  education treatment they can stay with  their families and maintain their  housing and think about you know every  step of the process you know those  opportunities to divert women away and  to also reinvest in our communities  instead of in incarceration to allow for  for more opportunities in the community  in so many of the communities that we  work with here at Avira Institute  very large amounts of  our budget goes towards incarcerating  people and you know I think we feel that  that that those resources would be  better spent creating more opportunities  in the community now Liz there will be  some who hear our conversation and  they'll say I agree with everything that  liz is saying we do need to be focusing  on all of those areas in order to reduce  female incarceration but shouldn't it  really apply to both men and women in  other words why is it that or not why is  it but do you feel that there is a  particular need to focus on reducing  female incarceration as a result of  certain types of crimes or economic  situations or whatever or are you sort  of merely pointing out that is it it has  increased more rapidly than male  incarceration but really we should be  making sure that we're treating  everybody in the same way and and I  asked this sort of because I know many  in the audience will hear our  conversation and that's the first  question I'll get percentage but they  are in jail and we are in this moment of  reform where local communities are  rethinking the way that they use their  jails and we absolutely encourage that  for all people both men and women there  should be opportunities to stay in the  community we're also being held  accountable but what we're seeing is  that as reforms are coming up and new  innovations are created such as  diversion programs they're not always  focusing on women and therefore aren't  reaching as women to the extent that  they're really reaching men so it's  important that we have conversations  overall about reducing the use of jails  but there there also be a specific focus  on women to make sure that any more  forms that are created are reaching them  and providing them opportunities as well  last issue I want to touch on if we had  to point to one specific law enforcement  initiative whose change might have the  the biggest impact do you come to war on  drugs and the status quo  there you know I think there are many  different factors I think the war on  drugs has certainly contributed  significantly to the number of women in  jails  I think communities are increasingly  just relying on their jails to provide  services that should be provided by the  community behavioral health mental  health substance use treatment there's  really you know the jails are the mental  health providers of last resort and we  need to rethink that and make sure that  resources are available in the community  to prevent people from ending up in jail  we've been speaking with Liz Savola  senior program associate at the Vera  Institute of Justice the report is  called overlooked women in jails in an  era of reform Alice thanks so much for  talking to us about this it's even my  pleasure

